# Database Access — Set-Based by Default

Database access must be set-based by default.

Never introduce repository, EF Core, Identity persistence, or other database-backed calls inside iteration when the operation can reasonably be performed using a bulk query.

Always inspect new database-access code for N+1 behavior.

N parallel queries using `Task.WhenAll` are still N queries and are not a valid N+1 fix.

Database query count should generally remain bounded as the number of processed items increases.

Do not over-optimize fixed O(1) query sequences merely to reduce query count.

Never call `SaveChanges` / `SaveChangesAsync` repeatedly inside a loop when the changes can be committed as one unit of work.

Treat lazy-loaded navigation access inside iteration as potential N+1 and verify the generated database access.

If persistence inside iteration is genuinely unavoidable, explicitly explain why a set-based/bulk operation is not appropriate before implementing it.

## Forbidden

- Repository / EF Core / Identity persistence calls inside `foreach` / `for` / `while` over collections
- `Task.WhenAll` over N per-item database queries as a substitute for bulk loading
- Lazy-loaded navigation access that triggers per-item queries
- Repeated `SaveChanges` / `SaveChangesAsync` inside a loop when one unit of work can commit all changes

## Preferred

- Collect and deduplicate ids, then one bulk query (`GetByIdsAsync` / `Where(ids.Contains(...))`)
- Dedicated set-based repository methods for use-case data shapes
- Projections when full entity graphs are unnecessary
- Stage all mutations, then a single `SaveChangesAsync`

## Acceptable

- Fixed O(1) sequential lookups in a single-item use case (existence check, load one entity, save once)
- Persistence inside iteration only when bulk is genuinely inappropriate, with an explicit written justification
